A task-specific dataset of out-of-distribution (OOD) examples is essential for validating the generalization of natural language processing (NLP) models on unseen data. Nevertheless, current methods for identifying OOD categories remain unsystematic, often relying on arbitrary criteria or prior knowledge. To address this gap, we propose a unified framework for categorizing ODF data and developing adaptive OOD detection algorithms without requiring additional training. By applying this framework to NLP models, we can improve their out-of-distribution performance and provide more accurate assessments of their limitations. Additionally, we can utilize this framework to create comprehensive taxonomies of OOD categories in NLP, which can guide future research and improve model evaluation practices by identifying and mitigating limitations in automated assessment methods. Our work evaluates the current effectiveness of NLP models on out-of-distribution data, highlighting areas for potential improvement and demonstrating the potential for adaptive NLP models to perform well on diverse data categories beyond their initial training. The proposed framework and results are available on our website at https://ood-categories.github.io/ and our code is available on GitHub at https://github.com/ooD-categories/ooD-categories.git. The dataset of annotated OOD categories can be accessed through the interface at https://ood-categories.github.io/. WD
